-- title:
--   Validity of the mixed integer relation certificates in block 22
-- statement:
--   Let B be the fixed 64-record list Couret.InterIA.SingletonNecessity.mixedChunk22 in MatchingCertificateData; its first and last stored index lists are [5, 0, 4, 3, 1, 2, 6] and [1, 0, 2, 5, 4, 6, 3], respectively. A certificate is a record (p,i,j,α), where p is a list of natural numbers, i,j are natural numbers, and α is a list of integers. Work in the additive group ℤ⁶, with standard basis e₀,…,e₅. Put ℓ₀=0 and ℓₖ=eₖ₋₁ for 1≤k≤6. Define the ordered seven-term lists L=(e₀,e₁,e₂,e₀+e₁,e₀+e₂,e₁+e₂,2e₀) and R=(e₃,e₄,e₅,e₃+e₄,e₃+e₅,e₄+e₅,2e₃). For a stored p=(p₀,…,p₆), define the ordered relation list r by concatenating (2(e₀−e₁),2(e₃−e₄),2(e₂−e₅)) with (Lₜ−Rₚₜ) for t=0,…,6 in increasing order; indices into L and R are zero-based. Every stored p has seven entries in {0,…,6}, so this gives ten relation rows with no out-of-range lookup. Then every certificate (p,i,j,α) in B passes valid true: i<j<7, the length of α equals the length of r (ten), and the integer vector identity ∑ₛ₌₀⁹ αₛrₛ=ℓᵢ−ℓⱼ holds in ℤ⁶. Equivalently, B.all (valid true)=true. These are all the checks in valid true; it performs no separate permutation or permutation-coverage test. The statement concerns this fixed list and has no variable input or additional hypothesis.
